Description: Show restored windows without activating them and open a new window for every launch and file.
 Text Editor restores its session by presenting each restored window, and GTK gives the activation token of the
 launch to the first window it shows, so with a valid token, as from the dock, GNOME switches to that window's
 workspace. A launch opens no new window, files opened from outside become tabs of the most recently used window,
 wherever it is, and while Text Editor runs, activating it presents an existing window.
 .
 Restored windows are now shown with GTK's automatic startup notification off, and a restored window whose files and
 drafts were all removed is discarded instead of showing a new draft. The request which restored the session then
 opens a window of its own, which receives the token: a new window with a draft for a launch, or one with the files.
 While Text Editor runs, activation and files opened from outside open a new window as well. A file which is open
 already is shown in its window, as before, and receives no new window.
 .
 Mutter focuses a window it shows when the window maps without an activation token, and it shows a restored window
 only after the window drew its first frame, several of them at once in an order of its own. The new window is
 therefore presented once mutter showed each restored window on a monitor, or 250 ms after the last of them drew its
 first frame, as one on another workspace is not shown, and after a second at the latest.
